---
description: Casual game-dev comments only where the code isn't obvious. no XML docs. short file header.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Code comments

Write like a teammate at 1am, not a library author. lowercase is fine. don't comment everything. skip a line if a decent game dev would get it from the code.

no em dashes. use a comma, period, or hyphen.

## File header

first thing in the type (after usings/namespace): one or two `//` lines saying what the script is / what it owns / what not to touch. not a formal banner.

## When to comment

- math / coordinate spaces / sign flips / timing that isn't obvious just by looking
- a gotcha or a fix so the next pass doesn't "correct" it back
- a weird Unity / pipeline thing (LateUpdate vs render, Y-down vs view-up, etc)

inline is good. one short line above or on the same line. don't plan comments for every function.

## Don't

- `/// <summary>` / `<param>` / XML doc tags. we don't use that.
- formal headers, perfect grammar, changelog essays
- restating the next line (`// increment i`)
- commenting every method, field, or obvious control flow just to "be complete"

## Examples

```csharp
// ❌ too formal / everywhere
/// <summary>
/// Applies the snap compensation offset to the destination rectangle.
/// </summary>
drawX += offset; // add offset to drawX

// ✅ enough
// nearest ignores sub-texel UVs, smooth pan comes from moving the dest
drawX += -error.x * px; // error is view-space (Y up); present matrix is Y-down
drawY += error.y * py;
```
